    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96855.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 6,
    "turn_text": "Well, I mean, a lot of the people that you talk with on a regular basis, I mean, they've got on their bucket list is to come to Washington DC, change the way that we do business for the better. And that was never on my bucket list. I was a business guy. you know, sign the front of a paycheck. And so was one of the few guys that when I got here, I thought all you did was you put people together in a room, you got the people that were nose and the people that were yes's and the people were maybe's and you find a found common ground. Well, that's not the way that Congress actually works. This is certainly not the way that Washington DC works. And I had come out of the business career because I was concerned about where our country was headed. Literally didn't necessarily want to be a member of Congress, but it was just concerned that there was not a strong leadership voice. Ran in 2011, started a campaign that lasted a little over 18 months. Tell me about that district. Had there been an incumbent that had retired? There was actually a Democrat holding the seat, Heath Schuller, which was probably, at that time, considered a blue dog Democrat, more of a conservative Democrat. They are non-existent. as you know, now on Capitol Hill. But Heath had served for three terms in Congress and was still there. I decided to run and redistricting had happened. And so he ended up retiring and his chief of staff ran for the position. So that was the first person that I actually ran, ran against. the people of western North Carolina decided to send me to congress and when I got here it was very different than I had imagined or at least thought that I",
