    "turn_text": "you know, first, you got to figure out where the bathroom. So, but I think And this is something, you know, always try to give a listener if I'm giving a speech or giving an interview like this, give the listener something that they won't hear anywhere else. And so probably one of the cold water in the face moments for me, was when I was actually working on a particular bill, and my freshman year literally had been there less than six months, worked on this idea for a bill, and I don't even remember the bill right now. But I had worked on this bill, went to some in leadership and They they like the idea. Yeah, this is a great idea. We think we're going to move it. So I'm thinking, all right, I'm going to get my name up there. I'm going to get this bill moved only to find out that a few weeks later, they were moving the bill, but it was no longer my bill. It had been given to someone else, a member from Kansas at that particular time. And I said, well, this member of Congress, it's not their bill. They didn't write it. Yeah, but they have something similar. And I said, well, not that I could find. And I found out very quickly that the way that power is consolidated here is doing favors for people. Even if that means taking original ideas from other people and trading it like you would an old coin.",
