    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96855.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 44,
    "turn_text": "Well, it does miss the point. I think what for me is whether it in that particular time was a John Boehner or, you know, as you know, the House is going through really who's going to be their speaker as the Republicans take over the majority in literally just a few days. It's putting in process where every member is able to advance their legislative priorities for their district. So if you represent a district where it's high tech, you want those members that can represent their district. and the high-tech industry, whether they're Republican or Democrats, to be able to advance that. Now, elections have consequences, and so if you have a Republican majority, hopefully those advances are more conservative. But what happens is, over time, we've concentrated so much power in the office of the Speaker. that most of the other committees, even the chairman of committees of jurisdiction, have very little power. And so we've got to fix that. If Congress is ever going to work, we have to fix it. And if people are out there listening right now and they serve in their state legislature, This doesn't even come close to the normal process in a state legislature where you offer an amendment, you get it heard, you figure out how to build coalitions, it's non-existent. Here it's all about convincing the speaker, well more accurately the speaker staff on what needs to advance in all of it transpires from there.",
