    "turn_text": "industry. season right now on whether it's going to be a CR or an omnibus. Well, this was a creative one in that we were doing a CR for part of it and an omnibus or a minibus. We called it the chromibus CR on the front of a bus and yet at the same time uh, many of us conservatives called it the crummy bus because they were putting all kinds of stuff in there that just didn't really make any sense. And so there was a rule on that. I can remember I was on the house floor talking to the chairman of the appropriations committee, how Rogers, the member from Kentucky. And as the chairman was there, uh, they were voting on the rule. Well, I look up, he looks up and he's this, this rule. It may not pass, and all of a sudden there was panic on the floor, and it's going every different direction. Well, a number of the conservatives had voted a different way on that rule. In fact, if all the conservatives had gotten together, that rule would not have passed. I went to a good friend of mine, Congressman Jim Jordan, Jim and I are close personal friends, and said, listen, if we're going to lose, we're going to lose in an organized fashion, let's get together. Let's create a group of people that are willing to vote as a block and vote together. He got four names. I got four names. We made those phone calls. And so the founding members, there was nine founding members of the House Freedom Caucus, but it came from a vote on which we didn't prevail. But we saw an opportunity to kind of band together and make a difference and create leverage points. And so a lot of that that actually happened in 2014-15. And many people think the House Freedom Caucus has been around for decades, but we're not even coming up on our 10-year anniversary yet.",
