    "turn_idx": 64,
    "turn_text": "I can tell you that, uh, You know, it's not something that I talk about on a regular basis. And it had nothing to do with John Boehner as a person. It had really to do with more of this infrastructure that was there that put so much power in one person's hands. I was looking for what one member could do. And so I was going through the Jefferson manual looking at privilege motions. And at that time, it was just really frustrated. I felt like, well, gosh, here I gave up a great career to come and serve in Congress. I'm not planning to serve more than three or four terms. I'm not going to be here long enough to make a difference. We've got to figure out a way to send a clear message. When anybody stands up for their people back home, this town and specifically many in leadership will start to really put a vice grip on on the jugular veins of members of Congress trying to get them to actually just become part of the establishment. And so I found this little-known motion to vacate. I think I Googled it and there was only a couple of references to motion to vacate the chair in on",
