    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96855.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 98,
    "turn_text": "You can have strong conservatives and you mentioned a group of conservatives that honestly this town has not corrupted. They're still strong conservatives willing to fight on behalf of their constituency. But yet the belief that anything is going to change in Washington DC is just not there. And so here was an outsider willing to say, I'm willing to take it on instead of saying I'm on the inside, I'm willing to take it on or I'm on the inside sort of I'm willing to take it on, but it's also one really was about bringing back pride to who we are as an American. The one thing that he tapped into, you know, who would have thought four words was, you know, make America great again was going to be this unbelievable selling point. And even less than that, who thought that those four words on a red cap with white printing that was not necessarily designed by a a marketing genius out of the finest PR firms in New York was going to take this town by storm. And not only did it take this town by storm, but you started to see red hats being worn by people of all different walks of life. And I think the other part of that is for that younger generation. They're looking for something or somebody who is authentic. And you could disagree with some of the style that President Trump had and then candidate Trump, but the very fact that he was willing to shake things up, they said, well, at least I can identify with someone who's willing to do that. So I think those are the lessons that are, that are",
