  {
    "episode_id": 96855,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96855.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 134,
    "turn_text": "I'm excited about the potential that a very marginal Republican majority hopefully can accomplish. And by that, I think that what happens is when you have to count every vote, you listen to every voice. And hopefully that's the first time that your listeners are hearing that. If you have to count every voice, every vote, You have to listen to every voice. Well, this is probably the first time that I know of in the last decade where every voice matters. And so I'm optimistic that there could be some systemic changes in the way that Congress does business. Hopefully those changes set a precedent. for not just Republican Congresses to follow, but for Democrat Congresses to follow. If you have a more open process for amendments and the ability to get bills to the floor, that's what a representative form of government's all about. So hopefully that happens. So I'm optimistic there. Non is optimistic that we're going to curtail spending. I am more optimistic that a limited government is starting to be something that men and women of all ages, and especially that newer generation come, are starting to embrace a more limited government is what Washington D.C. should be all about. And so I'm optimistic about both of those things.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "A very marginal Republican majority has the potential to accomplish meaningful change.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "An open process for amendments and access to the floor reflects representative governance.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
