    "turn_idx": 139,
    "turn_text": "Thank you again to Chief Meadows for coming on our Rinky Dink Little podcast. It does mean a lot to us that he would do so. And I feel slightly more comfortable having invited him on at this stage at the end of season two than I would have if we shook him down for one of those first five interviews because we've really come a long way. And I'm going to take this opportunity to brag a little bit on what American Moment has achieved here as we reach the Christmas of 2020. two. We're going to get pretty close to our two year anniversary here by the time you're listening to this and there'll be more reflections when we actually hit that date on February 24th of 2023. However, it's worth thinking about what happened this year exactly. I came to work every week. It felt like frustrated that we hadn't gotten enough done the week prior. Then you actually take a look back and you realize that's kind of bunk. We got a lot done this year and it wasn't me. It was my wonderful co-founders and our employees at American Moment who worked tirelessly through thick and thin, through good times and bad, through paternity leave and more to actually get things done in a nimble and effective way for our organization. This year we were covered by basically every single major media outlet there is, and we didn't have a PR team trying to solicit and press the flesh to get that done. It happened to its own accord because people thought what we were doing here was noteworthy, whether it was the New York Times, the Washington Post, Politico, and Axios, the Atlantic. or any other publication, they found something to pay attention to and notice here at American moment this year. Why? Because we were doing what we said we would do. We've built this network of over a thousand strong now, talented, capable, committed young people who are going to staff presidential administration, congressional offices, and public policy organizations in the years to come. Those people have arrived, and more often than not, every person we send to the Hill in this case is the youngest in their category. We've not even realized it sometimes. How impressive the people that come through our doors are, but we are extremely blessed that We aren't making amazing people, we're finding them, and we're helping provide them the professional connections and the opportunities they need in order to be the best versions of themselves, serving the worldview that we care about here at American Moment. And that worldview remains as essential to what we're doing on a day-to-day basis as ever. One of the things that I'm sure some of you can imagine is that Washington has this acculturating effect to the people who go to it. And one of the risks that we were always cognizant of is that we could easily find ourselves sort of assimilated into the traditional way of thinking about certain issues on the right. That didn't happen. I want to talk a little bit about a specific case in which we were able to stand a thwart the mob here in DC and take a different perspective. When that war in Ukraine started and March of this year in February, I guess, there were a ton of organizations that were keeping their powder very dry. They weren't really saying much about what they thought. If anything, they were parroting the party line and we're escalating ourselves dramatically into a full tilt conflict with the nuclear armed power. We took a different tack. We worked with our partners at TAC, the American conservative magazine. We hosted a conference on a 40-day turnaround period called Up from Chaos, conserving American security. And in that conference, we brought the full tilt hard core take at the time that maybe we're going too far. Ultimately, everything that was said at that conference is aged exceptionally well. Now, you can draw a direct line from the coalitions and communities and opportunities for new leaders to rise, whether they were members of Congress or businessmen. at that conference to a growing consensus on the right that a more realistic and restrained foreign policy must be essential to our governing agenda moving forward. We were able to expand far beyond just that event to more complicated programming that helped showcase our ability to put together real professional opportunities for the people in our cadre. You know, in year one, we just had our Fellowship for American Statecraft in year two. We added twin programs on either side of that fellowship, one for people more junior. There's hundreds of interns that get brought to DC every single year. We wanted to meet them and find the ones that had what it took. We spun up AM Friday. It's a series of 10 lectures across the summer and then also across the spring where we brought in the congressional interns from all the best offices and some of the less conservative ones as well, peeling through them, finding the ones who shared our beliefs and taking them to new heights, being able to help them in a differential way that the mass of opportunities in DC aren't necessarily able to do. On the other side of things, we launched Foundations of American Statecraft, a more advanced series of programming across different issue areas, as it makes sense, designed to help credential already, junior staff into taking on more senior roles. The first one of those was Foundations of American Statecraft, Conflict, Foreign Policy, and Diplomacy. It was on foreign policy and went exceptionally well. That will return here in just a few short weeks. Look for the application on our website. And then we did foundations of American statecraft congressional process and paradigm shift to retreat and subsequent lecture series we hosted where we tried to prepare our cadre for work on the hill in this new Congress working for fantastic members in middle of the road ones alike to make sure that they can be maximally effective on day one. We're extremely, extremely grateful for everything we've been able to achieve this year. This podcast is sort of the tip of the iceberg in that it's the 10% you see. And it's gotten to be a pretty big iceberg at that. Almost any guest we asked to come on comes on. And so there's been just this absolutely fantastic, you know, generative effect of having people on this show that you guys like to listen to. You share that show with your friends and they share it with other people and the steady stream of incoming people and talent that come into our network. through this show and the coalitional opportunities were able to build through it. Thank you, as always, for listening. Thank you for being such good friends of American Moment. If there's anything we can do for you, send me an email at Sir Robin American Moment or Nick at American Moment, and we will do our best to serve the audience of this show and the community of deeply committed, high character",
